\newlabel{sec:res-alloc}{{6.3}{12}{What allocation is worth}{subsection.6.3}{}}
\newlabel{sec:res-lik}{{6.3}{12}{What allocation is worth}{subsection.6.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Stopping benchmarks and model-free allocation baselines (digital twin). \textbf  {(a)} On the same 300 retrievals as Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:actual}: verified stopping against plans that do not stop and commit a fixed budget. Success is the share of retrievals the budget completes (cluster-bootstrap 95\% CI); reads per completed retrieval assume that a failed attempt is repeated with a fresh budget (budget/success). \textbf  {(b)} Exploratory baselines on \emph  {regenerated} read streams (DT4DDS is not seed-deterministic), each paired against round-robin re-run on the same streams; mean reads over successful retrievals, relative difference on jointly successful retrievals. Per-read stop applies the stopping test after every read instead of every 50. No retrieval in (b) returned a wrong file. Thousands of reads.}}{13}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r9base}{{4}{13}{Stopping benchmarks and model-free allocation baselines (digital twin). \textbf {(a)} On the same 300 retrievals as Table~\ref {tab:actual}: verified stopping against plans that do not stop and commit a fixed budget. Success is the share of retrievals the budget completes (cluster-bootstrap 95\% CI); reads per completed retrieval assume that a failed attempt is repeated with a fresh budget (budget/success). \textbf {(b)} Exploratory baselines on \emph {regenerated} read streams (DT4DDS is not seed-deterministic), each paired against round-robin re-run on the same streams; mean reads over successful retrievals, relative difference on jointly successful retrievals. Per-read stop applies the stopping test after every read instead of every 50. No retrieval in (b) returned a wrong file. Thousands of reads}{table.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The complete primary comparison repeated under each likelihood setting: the primary $\rho _U{=}0.1$, the value the corrected selection picks ($\rho _U{=}0.03$, Section\nonbreakingspace \ref {sec:res-calib}) and the binomial ($\rho _U{=}0$). All three use the full design (30 syntheses $\times $ 10 read orders per policy) and the primary prior width $s{=}0.5$. Entries are the paired difference in mean reads against round-robin on jointly successful retrievals, per cent, with cluster-bootstrap 95\% intervals; negative is cheaper than round-robin. Round-robin never evaluates the posterior, so it is bit-identical across the three settings and is the common reference. Success probability is unchanged by $\rho _U$ in every scenario.}}{14}{table.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r6lik}{{5}{14}{The complete primary comparison repeated under each likelihood setting: the primary $\rho _U{=}0.1$, the value the corrected selection picks ($\rho _U{=}0.03$, Section~\ref {sec:res-calib}) and the binomial ($\rho _U{=}0$). All three use the full design (30 syntheses $\times $ 10 read orders per policy) and the primary prior width $s{=}0.5$. Entries are the paired difference in mean reads against round-robin on jointly successful retrievals, per cent, with cluster-bootstrap 95\% intervals; negative is cheaper than round-robin. Round-robin never evaluates the posterior, so it is bit-identical across the three settings and is the common reference. Success probability is unchanged by $\rho _U$ in every scenario}{table.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Digital-twin end-to-end retrieval, baseline unless stated. \textbf  {(a)} For each policy, the reads a retrieval actually \emph  {consumes}: filled circles are the mean over 300 retrievals with a 95\% cluster-bootstrap interval over syntheses, open squares the 0.99-quantile of the same retrievals. The two vertical lines are the capacity a plan that does \emph  {not} stop must \emph  {reserve} to succeed on 99\% of retrievals: dotted for uniform fixed depth, dashed for the best static plan. The horizontal distance from a filled circle to a vertical line is the saving verified stopping provides; the distance between circles is the (much smaller) allocation effect. \textbf  {(b)} Allocation effect alone: each bar is the paired difference in mean reads against round-robin with the same verified stopping, computed on the retrievals in which both policies succeeded, with 95\% cluster-bootstrap intervals; negative is cheaper than round-robin. Information-greedy is annotated rather than plotted because it runs off the scale. Scenarios in which no retrieval succeeded (photolithographic, two failed containers) have no paired statistic and are omitted.}}{14}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dt}{{2}{14}{Digital-twin end-to-end retrieval, baseline unless stated. \textbf {(a)} For each policy, the reads a retrieval actually \emph {consumes}: filled circles are the mean over 300 retrievals with a 95\% cluster-bootstrap interval over syntheses, open squares the 0.99-quantile of the same retrievals. The two vertical lines are the capacity a plan that does \emph {not} stop must \emph {reserve} to succeed on 99\% of retrievals: dotted for uniform fixed depth, dashed for the best static plan. The horizontal distance from a filled circle to a vertical line is the saving verified stopping provides; the distance between circles is the (much smaller) allocation effect. \textbf {(b)} Allocation effect alone: each bar is the paired difference in mean reads against round-robin with the same verified stopping, computed on the retrievals in which both policies succeeded, with 95\% cluster-bootstrap intervals; negative is cheaper than round-robin. Information-greedy is annotated rather than plotted because it runs off the scale. Scenarios in which no retrieval succeeded (photolithographic, two failed containers) have no paired statistic and are omitted}{figure.caption.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Ablation of the three modelling choices (digital twin; paired mean reads against round-robin on jointly successful retrievals, per cent, negative is cheaper than round-robin). $\theta $: one efficiency multiplier per container (the primary model); $\theta {+}d$: efficiency and a dropout fraction. $\rho _U$ is the beta-binomial correlation and $s$ the prior width; the primary configuration is the first column. On the baseline, relaxing any one of the three choices removes most of the penalty, but not all of it for every rule: single relaxations leave the rules between 4\% below and 13\% above round-robin (tail-aware keeps $+$10\% with the dropout model alone). Blank: not run. Cluster-bootstrap intervals for every cell are in \texttt  {results/r4\_ablation.csv}; those the text relies on are quoted there.}}{15}{table.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:r4abl}{{6}{15}{Ablation of the three modelling choices (digital twin; paired mean reads against round-robin on jointly successful retrievals, per cent, negative is cheaper than round-robin). $\theta $: one efficiency multiplier per container (the primary model); $\theta {+}d$: efficiency and a dropout fraction. $\rho _U$ is the beta-binomial correlation and $s$ the prior width; the primary configuration is the first column. On the baseline, relaxing any one of the three choices removes most of the penalty, but not all of it for every rule: single relaxations leave the rules between 4\% below and 13\% above round-robin (tail-aware keeps $+$10\% with the dropout model alone). Blank: not run. Cluster-bootstrap intervals for every cell are in \texttt {results/r4\_ablation.csv}; those the text relies on are quoted there}{table.caption.8}{}}
\newlabel{sec:res-extra}{{6.3}{15}{What allocation is worth}{figure.caption.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Digital-twin end-to-end retrieval (DT4DDS; primary evidence). Per scenario and policy: share of retrievals whose output file is byte-identical to the stored file (no retrieval returned a wrong file), mean reads per retrieval (thousands; cluster-bootstrap 95\% CI over syntheses), the 0.99-quantile q99 (the budget to reserve), and the paired relative difference in mean reads against round-robin with verified stopping on retrievals where both succeeded (95\% CI). \textbf  {Static plans are costed here as \emph  {reservations}}: they read fixed proportions until decodable and must commit their q99 on every retrieval, so their mean cost equals their q99, and their proportions use per-container efficiencies measured in hindsight on the same scenario. Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:actual} reports the same plans run \emph  {with} verified stopping, where their consumed mean is far lower; the two tables answer different questions and their static rows are not comparable.}}{16}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:dt}{{7}{16}{Digital-twin end-to-end retrieval (DT4DDS; primary evidence). Per scenario and policy: share of retrievals whose output file is byte-identical to the stored file (no retrieval returned a wrong file), mean reads per retrieval (thousands; cluster-bootstrap 95\% CI over syntheses), the 0.99-quantile q99 (the budget to reserve), and the paired relative difference in mean reads against round-robin with verified stopping on retrievals where both succeeded (95\% CI). \textbf {Static plans are costed here as \emph {reservations}}: they read fixed proportions until decodable and must commit their q99 on every retrieval, so their mean cost equals their q99, and their proportions use per-container efficiencies measured in hindsight on the same scenario. Table~\ref {tab:actual} reports the same plans run \emph {with} verified stopping, where their consumed mean is far lower; the two tables answer different questions and their static rows are not comparable}{table.caption.9}{}}
